Introduction ❑ The NEPSY-II is a neuropsychological battery designed for children aged 3 – 16 years. ❑ It was designed to assess six cognitive domains: Attention/Executive Functioning (A/EF), Language (L), Sensorimotor (S), Visuospatial Processing (VS), Memory and Learning (M&L), and Social Perception (SP). ❑ The test authors report that the NEPSY-II is useful for both decision-making and intervention planning. ❑ Although based on Luria’s theory – originally developed for adults – its structural validity in children remains debated. ❑ Few structural analyses have examined the proposed domain structure, and no aggregate domain scores are provided in the manual. ▪ As such, the underlying structure of the NEPSY-II remains contested in the literature. ▪ Interpretation is limited to the subtest level; however, no subtest should be interpreted in isolation, as each engages multiple cognitive processes and may overlap with several domains.
...
❑ This study examined the internal structure of the NEPSY-II across three age groups (5–6, 6–7, and 13–16 years). ❑ Due to ongoing uncertainty regarding the NEPSY-II’s theoretical model and limited structural validation, it remains unclear what its scores truly reflect. ❑ Since individual subtest scores should not be interpreted in isolation, identifying which tests cluster within the same domain is essential. ▪ This will clarify how the scores can be meaningfully interpreted and applied in clinical contexts. ❑ Exploratory Factor Analysis (EFA) and Psychometric Network Analysis (EGA, bootEGA) were used to examine the structure and interpretability of the NEPSY-II scores.
