URL: https://riikkasleasweeklyportfolio.wordpress.com/2022/03/05/hofstedes-cultural-dimensions-south-korea-finland/
South Korea is a slightly more hierarchical society than Finland. This may influence the way business is done in the country, as well as the expectations South Korean employees hold towards a company’s culture. A company located in Finland with its lower hierarchical structures is used to allowing its employees to interact with managers on somewhat equal footing. For instance, it may be common to address higher-ups with their first names. In contrast, more hierarchical business cultures are common that, for example, include an intricate system of reporting to higher-ups within the country.

A company based in Finland wanting to expand its business to South Korea must take such differences into account to avoid misunderstandings and confusion among its South Korean employees. In essence, the company must decide whether to implement its current internal structure into its operations in South Korea or adapt its company culture to meet South Korean expectations.
...
The gap between Finland and South Korea in terms of their degree of individualism is significant, with the former being considered a fairly individualistic society and the latter a collectivistic one. When it comes to business, this may be reflected in the employee’s mindset towards their work and responsibilities. For instance, while a Finnish employee may choose to miss a deadline as they are ill, a South Korean worker might meet that deadline despite their illness because they do not want to let down their manager who entrusted them with said task.

More generally speaking, this dimension affects the way an individual employee sees themselves within the company – either as one individual striving to be successful (Finland) within a company or as part of the entity that is the company (South Korea). In practi…

[2] Geert Hofstede's Dimensions of Culture and Edward T. Hall's Time Orientations
URL: https://tamas.com/sites/default/files/2019-06/Hofstede_Hall.pdf
Hofstede's "dimensions of culture" were derived mainly from his extensive organizational 
anthropology research in the late 1970s and early 1980s – the scores are general comparisons of 
values in the countries and regions he studied and can vary greatly within each country. Although 
Hofstede's work is somewhat dated and has rightly been criticized on a number of grounds the 
dimensions are useful in understanding that members of various societies are likely to behave in 
different ways in a given situation. 
Power Distance Index (PDI) is defined as “the extent to which the less powerful members 
of institutions and organisations within a country expect and accept that power is distributed 
unequally”. (Hofstede, 1994, p. 28) A High Power Distance ranking indicates that inequalities of 
power and wealth exist within the society and that the less powerful members of the society accept 
this situation. A Low Power Distance ranking indicates the society de-emphasizes the differences 
between citizen's power and wealth. In these societies equality and opportunity for everyone is 
stressed. 
Individualism (IDV) Hofstede defines this dimension as follows: “individualism pertains to 
societies in which the ties between individuals are loose: everyone is expected to look after himself or 
herself and his or her immediate family." A High Individualism ranking indicates that individuality and 
individual rights are paramount within the society. A Low Individualism ranking typifies societies of a 
more collectivist nature with close ties among its members. In these societies "…people from birth 
onwards are integrated into strong, cohesive in-groups, which throughout people’s lifetime continue to 
protect them in exchange for unquestioning loyalty.” (Hofstede, 1994, p. 51) 
