Abstract The COVID-19 pandemic forced students from Mexico and the world to migrate to an online mode of education. In this context, Learning Management Systems (LMS) gained importance. From which, the most widely used in Mexico is Google Classroom (GC). However, there is little information on the acceptance and use of this LMS by students in this country. Therefore, this study used a variation of the second version of the structural model of the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT2) to determine the degree of influence that the factors defined in the theory have and their relationship with the behavioral intention to use GC. For this reason, this study applied surveys to 102 students 6 th semester high school students from Colegio Anahuac Garibaldi (CAG) and corroborated the correlations of the factors that determine the behavioral intention to use GC. Data analysis was performed using Partial Least Squares Structural Equations (PLS-SEM). In this sense, it was found that the direct determinant of intention to use CG was habit, followed by performance expectancy and social influence. On the other hand, effort expectancy, facilitating conditions and hedonic motivation are not direct determinants of behavioral intention to use CG. However, facilitating conditions are indirect determinants through habit, while hedonic motivation is a predictor of intention to use, through performance expectancy,
