Academic writing instruction constitutes a fundamental component of English language programs globally, serving as a critical mechanism for developing students' disciplinary knowledge, analytical reasoning, and scholarly communication skills. Within English as a Foreign Language (EFL) context, however, mastering academic writing presents formidable challenges (Van Le et al., 2025). Chinese university students, despite extensive exposure to English education throughout their schooling, continue to encounter substantial difficulties with key dimensions of academic writing including lexical precision, syntactic complexity, argumentation structure, and academic voice construction. These persistent challenges have motivated educators and researchers to explore innovative pedagogical approaches that transcend traditional teacher-dominated, examination-oriented instruction toward more interactive, learner-centered methodologies emphasizing collaborative knowledge construction and peer-mediated learning.

Among these pedagogical innovations, peer feedback has attracted considerable scholarly and practical attention as a promising mechanism for enhancing writing proficiency, fostering critical literacy, and developing learner autonomy (Lee & Mao, 2024; Zhang & Mao, 2023). Rooted in sociocultural and constructivist learning theories, peer feedback reconceptualizes students as co-constructors of knowledge who engage in reciprocal evaluation, critique, and mutual support through collaborative textual interact…

