Language teachers who adopt a process approach to writing instruction often involve their classes in peer review — an activity in which stu dents read and comment upon one another’s compositions. In the case of first language learners the pedagogical benefits of peer review have been well documented, but researchers investigating the benefits for second language learners have had mixed results. While there is some evidence that they also benefit from peer review, other studies suggest that some learners have difficulty providing feedback because they are reluctant to criticize their classmates’ work. This paper reports on a survey conducted with a 1 st year writing class at Ritsumeikan University to ascertain students’ attitudes to different formats of peer review. Specifically, it sought to establish whether there is any merit in anonymous peer review. It was found that when students provided their feedback anonymously they were less worried about offending the writer, which in turn appeared to result in more useful feedback. It is suggested that the anonymous format provides a particularly effective way for Japanese students to conduct peer review.
