[3] Momos advises Zeus: Changing representations of 'Cypria' fragment 1 - ORA - Oxford University Research Archive
URL: https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid:a8c2061b-0bee-4f57-aa2d-0de7f1a2d306
## Momos advises Zeus: Changing representations of 'Cypria' fragment 1

Abstract: This paper investigates the importance of context for assessing fragment one of the ‘Cypria’, one of the poems belonging to an ‘Epic Cycle’ that – along with the Iliad and Odyssey – told the story of the war at Troy. With the exception of the Homeric epics, these poems come down to us in pieces, in the form of mutilated quotations, assorted testimonia and a later summary by Proclus, all of which, by offering brief glimpses of a wider epic world, tend to be mined for glossing the Homeric poems or else for reconstructing the ‘original’ lost narratives. This paper rejects the pursuit of an archetype in favour of tracing the changing representations of the ‘Cypria’ fragment one and exploring the impact of reception on its interpretation. It makes three claims: first, that, from what we can tell from fragment one itself, the ‘Cypria’ potentially belonged to a wider epic world that shared common terminology, themes and strategies with the Homeric poems; second, that the second-century AD summary of Proclus, by means of which the plot of the ‘Cypria’ has come down to us, critically recasts it in relation to, and at the service of, reading Homer; third, that the ‘D-scholion’ to the Iliad, which is our actual source for fragment one of the ‘Cypria’, already betrays the gravitational pull of the Homeric poems, around which the rest of the epic cycle comes to revolve.

[4] The Cypria
URL: https://brill.com/downloadpdf/view/journals/yago/10/1/article-p1_1.pdf
Among the poems conventionally grouped under the label “Epic Cycle”, the 
Cypria occupies a structurally privileged and methodologically vexing posi 
tion. Structurally, it narrates the long prehistory of the Trojan War—its remote 
divine “causes”, the assembling of the expedition, and initial phase of the 
campaign—only to deliver, at its terminus, a handover to the Iliad so tight that 
it has repeatedly invited the language of “prequel” or “introduction” to Homer. 
Methodologically, however, the actual poem is lost: what we possess are (i) a 
relatively detailed prose plot-summary transmitted under Proclus’ name, and 
(ii) a heterogeneous collection of testimonia and fragments quoted for anti 
quarian, grammatical, or exegetical purposes. Any reading of the Cypria must 
therefore be a composite act: reconstruction of narrative sequence; evaluation 
of witnesses whose agendas are rarely neutral; and, finally, interpretation of a 
poetic object that we cannot access in continuous and full form. 
As a result, scholarship on the Cypria has never been only “about” the Cypria. 
It has repeatedly served as a test case for larger disputes: the historical relation 
ship between Homer and the poets of the Epic Cycle; the legitimacy and limits
...
tion of results, but as a sequence of shifting methodological settlements—each 
defining anew what the Cypria is allowed to be. 
What follows is a selective survey of representative contributions that have 
shaped the modern study of the Cypria, with particular attention to the poem’s 
relation to the Iliad and to the evidentiary problem posed by Proclus’ summary 
and the fragment-tradition.
