In Book 9 of the Iliad, at the end of his long, impassioned rejection of Agamemnon and the
Greek cause, Achilles makes two curious statements. Each deals, in its own way, with future
events. The first is an explanation of Achilles’s own fate:
...
For my mother, silver-footed Thetis, tells me that I bear a dual fate towards the end of death. The first—if I should stay here and fight around the city of Troy, my return home is lost, but my glory will be undying. And the second—if I should return home to the beloved land of my fathers, my great glory is lost, but a long life will be mine, and the end of death will not find me quickly (Il. 9.410-416).2

