The surviving fragments of the Cypria number approximately twenty-seven in Martin L. West's 2003 edition, encompassing a range from isolated hexameter lines to extended passages of up to several dozen verses, with some consisting of direct quotations and others of paraphrased excerpts or testimonia. [20] These remnants are drawn chiefly from citations in late antique and Byzantine grammarians, such as Eustathius of Thessalonica's commentary on Homer, mythographers including Apollodorus of Athens in his Bibliotheca, and fragmentary papyri, notably a set of late 2nd-century AD scraps from Oxyrhynchus (P.Oxy. 3829) that preserve narrative details in prose form. [3] [21] Fragments are classified into two main categories: argumenta or summaries in prose, which outline narrative elements without verse, and authentic poetic fragments in dactylic hexameter that represent the original epic composition. [20] A prominent example of the former is Fragment 1, the comprehensive plot summary preserved in Proclus's Chrestomathy (via Photius's Bibliotheca), which structures the poem across eleven books from Zeus's scheme to the Greek fleet's departure for Troy. Among the hexameter fragments, notable instances include Fragment 9, directly quoted in scholia to Homer's Iliad (on 11.67), which depicts Zeus consulting with Themis (or Momos in variant traditions) about depopulating the earth through war. [3] Another key piece is Fragment 27, a paraphrase in Herodotus's Histories (2.116–117), referencing the Cypria's depiction of Paris's judgment among the goddesses and his subsequent journey with Helen. Challenges to authenticity arise from potential interpolations or erroneous attributions in the transmitting sources, as ancient compilers sometimes conflated cyclic epics or added explanator…

