ManyChat's pricing is built around active contacts: the number of unique people your account interacts with through messages or automations each month. At the time of writing, the published plans look like this:

| Plan | Monthly price | Active contacts included |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Free | $0 | 25 |
| Essential | $14 | 250 |
| Pro | $29 | 2,500 |
| Business | $69 | 7,500 |
| Advanced | $139 | 25,000 |

Go over your included contacts and a per-contact overage applies, from around $0.10 per contact on Essential down to fractions of a cent on Advanced. Annual billing discounts these numbers. AI conversation features are bundled into Pro and above.

The structure matters more than the exact numbers. Because you pay per person who interacts, your bill is tied to your reach: a reel that goes viral and pulls in thousands of keyword comments is exactly the moment your costs jump. Creators in forums regularly describe upgrading mid-month after a post took off. That is not a hidden fee, it is how contact-based pricing works, but you should budget for success, not for your average month.

