[2] Improving Health Care Access and Referral Continuity via
URL: https://www.villagereach.org/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Lagos-Telehealth-Case-Study-Improving-Health-Care-Access-and-Referral-Continuity-via-Telehealth-Hotlines-and-Kiosks-in-Lagos-Nigeria.pdf
Lagos State, Nigeria, tested digital-first care models to improve access to primary health services for under-reached communities through telehealth hotlines and kiosks. The project was carried out by the Lagos government in collaboration with the Lagos State Health Management Agency (LASHMA), VillageReach, Solina Center for International Development and Research (SCIDaR) and private providers (HealthConnect 247, mDoc and mPharma), with support from the Gates Foundation. Many people in Lagos faced persistent barriers to care, including transport costs, time constraints and the high cost of tests and medicines, often delaying or preventing them from seeking care altogether.

Through the project, three models were introduced: a toll-free hotline, market-based telehealth kiosks and community pharmacy-based kiosks. These models created more convenient and trusted first points of contact with the health system, bringing care closer to where people lived and worked and, in some cases, removing the need to travel entirely. They also supported early engagement with care, with most interactions focused on health information and assessment.

These changes improved early access to care and encouraged people to seek help sooner rather than delay. At the same time, the project highlighted that access to consultation alone is not sufficient. Referral completion, access to diagnostics and medicines and insurance linkage remain key gaps. The result is a clearer understanding of both the value of digital-first care in expanding access and the system-level investments needed to support a complete continuum of care.
