According to the World Health Organization, sub-Saharan Africa has fewer than one pharmacist per 10,000 population in most countries, leaving millions of patients without access to professional medication counselling. This research assessed the feasibility and preliminary outcomes of a telepharmacy service linking clinical pharmacists at a central hub in Kigali, Rwanda, with four remote health facilities in rural districts lacking on-site pharmacy professionals. A mixed-methods feasibility design was used over a six-month pilot period from May to October 2023. A total of 326 patients across the four sites received remote medication counselling via video call or mobile messaging. Feasibility indicators included session completion rate, technology reliability, patient satisfaction, and medication adherence at three months. The session completion rate was 87.4%. Technology failures (dropped calls, connectivity loss) occurred in 14.7% of sessions but were resolved through callback or switch to SMS in most cases. Mean counselling duration was 18.3 minutes via telepharmacy compared with 22.6 minutes for in-person sessions at the hub (p = 0.02). Patient satisfaction scores averaged 4.1 out of 5. Medication adherence at three months was 74.8% in the telepharmacy group versus 71.3% in a matched in-person comparator group (p = 0.38). Follow-up completion was lower in the telepharmacy arm (68.2% vs 81.4%, p<0.01). These results suggest that telepharmacy is feasible in rural Rwanda and can deliver comparable adherence outcomes, though follow-up engagement requires targeted strategies to match in-person levels.
