Accessibility standards and guidelines like Section 508 and WCAG define whether content can be accessed—but they do not fully capture how much effort it takes to use.

For screen reader users, many “accessible” experiences remain cognitively demanding. Tasks that should be simple—finding information, completing forms, navigating course materials—often require excessive navigation, memory, and interpretation. These challenges are especially pronounced in higher education, where users interact with complex systems such as learning management systems, PDFs, and multi-step institutional workflows.

This session builds on the Screen Reader Ropes Course, a structured navigation and testing model that reflects how screen reader users actually experience digital content. We introduce an evolved version of this model that reframes accessibility through the lens of cognitive load. Using the familiar phases of Arrival, Orientation, Survey, Interaction, and Completion, we examine how each step contributes to user effort—and where that effort becomes a barrier. This session builds on prior navigation-based approaches but introduces a new lens focused on user effort and sustainability.

