[4] A Comparative Evaluation of Accessible Input Modalities for Mobile Mathematics Learning in Visually Impaired Students (2026-09-07T00:00:00.000Z)
URL: https://exa.ai/library/publication/t52dqv29zmc
The effectiveness of accessible mathematics applications for visually impaired learners depends on the efficiency of their input modalities, yet empirical comparisons across modalities remain limited. Existing systems are often evaluated in isolation, providing limited evidence to guide interaction design under screen-reader-mediated conditions. This paper presents a pilot-scale controlled within-subject evaluation of four accessible input modalities—multiple-choice (MCQ), numeric keypad, tap-based counter, and shake-based counter—implemented in a mobile mathematics learning application. A study with N = 8 visually impaired participants is complemented by a simulation study with N = 11 blindfolded sighted participants to examine the consistency of observed trends. Results indicate that input modality has a statistically significant effect on task completion time (p < 0.001), mental demand (p = 0.045), effort (p = 0.039), and frustration (p = 0.011). Counter-based modalities demonstrate consistently higher efficiency and lower cognitive load, while navigation-dependent input introduces measurable interaction overhead. As a pilot study, these findings provide early empirical evidence for accessible input design and suggest that interaction structure is a primary determinant of performance in non-visual mathematics interaction.

