Abstract: Most digital services still assume that the user can see a screen. For people with a visual impairment, that assumption turns routine tasks such as reading a message, checking a bus time or operating an appliance into tasks that depend on another person. This paper describes the design of an AI-based voice interaction system that lets a visually impaired user complete such tasks through spoken conversation alone. The system chains voice activity detection, noise-robust speech recognition, intent and entity extraction, a context-aware dialogue manager and neural speech synthesis, and exposes them through a mobile client and a Python backend. Three design decisions shape the work: answers are kept short and put the most important information first; every action that changes something is confirmed by voice before it runs; and a reduced offline mode keeps basic commands working when connectivity is poor. We also set out an evaluation protocol covering word error rate, intent accuracy, task completion, response latency, the System Usability Scale and the NASA Task Load Index, to be carried out with visually impaired volunteers. The paper reports the architecture, the reasoning behind it and the measurement plan; Table I lists the fields in which pilot results are recorded.
