Grocery shopping depends strongly on visual information, whether it’s identifying a product, reading ingredients and allergen warnings or checking an expiry date. AI-based visual assistance applications can make some of this information more accessible to blind and visually impaired people, but using these tools in an actual supermarket also brings challenges that aren’t only related to how accurately a product can be recognized. This thesis explores how interaction design can better support blind and visually impaired people in making safe and independent choices while grocery shopping.The research combined an in-store evaluation of three AI visual assistance applications, a contextual inquiry with a blind participant and a digital ethnography of online blind and visually impaired communities. The thematic network analysis identified the following four connected challenges: embodied interaction constraints, unreliable AI output, inaccessible error communication, and recovery being left to the user. These findings showed how users often compensate for technological limitations through physical adaptation, repeated extra verification and their own recovery strategies.A co-design session explored alternative interactions including gesture-based commands, safety-critical information, haptic feedback and guided recovery. These ideas were brought together in a proposed user journey and a low-fidelity prototype.The project suggests that supporting independence requires more than improving AI recognition. Interaction design can make system behaviour clearer, support recovery and reduce the burden placed on users when technology fa…

