URL: https://journal.chestnet.org/article/S0012-3692(18)31561-7/fulltext
PURPOSE: Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF) is a progressive, inevitably fatal, chronic lung disease. Converging evidence implicates cellular senescence in IPF disease pathogenesis. Selectively ablating senescent cells in vivo using dasatinib and quercetin (D+Q) alleviates symptoms of IPF and frailty in mouse models, but this has yet to be tested in humans. We conducted a two-center, open-label pilot study of D+Q administration in patients with IPF. The primary objective was to evaluate feasibility and acceptability of intermittent D+Q and study assessments in IPF patients. Secondary objectives were to gain preliminary estimates of safety and tolerability and gain initial insight on exploratory functional outcomes.

METHODS: We conducted a 3-week open label study of intermittent dasatinib (100mg/day) and quercetin (1250mg/day) in IPF patients, using a dosing schedule of 3 consecutive days D+Q per week (i.e., 9 total patient administered dosing days). The primary endpoints were treatment-specific retention and adherence rates, and proportion of planned assessments completed. Key secondary endpoints were adverse events reported and tolerability of drug regimen. Exploratory endpoints were the change from baseline in: 1) physical function: 6-minute walk distance (6MWD), grip strength, short physical performance battery (eSPPB); 2) spirometry; 3) lab-based frailty index; 4) patient reported quality of life, respiratory health, and fatigue.

