By establishing a limit or “cap” on total emissions and a market for entities to “trade” emissions allowances, “cap-and-trade” creates an economic incentive to reduce emissions. California’s cap-and-trade program is part of the state’s broader strategy to reduce (i.e., mitigate) GHG emissions and meet its GHG targets. The California legislature has established GHG targets for 2020, 2030, and 2045—and requires the state to achieve net-zero GHG emissions, a status where human-caused GHG emissions to the atmosphere are balanced by removal of GHGs from the atmosphere. California’s program is often referred to as the first “economy-wide” GHG cap-and-trade program due to its broad scope: It covers the majority—approximately 80%—of statewide GHG emissions from multiple sectors of the economy, including electric power, industry, transportation, and buildings.
