### Latency and Throughput

BentoML achieves low latency through adaptive batching and efficient request handling. In benchmarks with ResNet50, BentoML handles 1000+ requests per second with p95 latency under 50ms on modest hardware. The Python-based runtime is optimized but still subject to GIL limitations for CPU-bound operations.

Seldon Core's performance depends heavily on your implementation choices. Using pre-packaged servers (like MLServer or Triton) gives you production-grade performance. Custom Python servers might introduce overhead from additional container layers and Kubernetes networking. The graph execution model adds minimal latency (~1-2ms) when chaining components.

KServe leverages high-performance runtimes like Triton Inference Server for GPU workloads and TorchServe for PyTorch models. Scale-to-zero via Knative introduces cold start latency (typically 2-5 seconds), which matters for bursty workloads but less for steady traffic.
...
- Latency targets: p50, p95, p99 latencies under expected load
- Throughput requirements: requests per second the system must handle
- Resource efficiency: cost per 1000 predictions or similar metric
- Deployment velocity: time from model training to production deployment
- Operational overhead: engineer hours per week maintaining the platform

