They process more than 100 million transactions for over 20 million monthly users. However, the scalability of their platform presents opportunities for users to defraud their services and undermine their user experience.

Organized crime syndicates use advanced technology to circumvent the system and reap rewards. The two major challenges they face are GPS fraud (location spoofing) and incentive fraud.

GPS spoofing occurs when a driver falsely claims they are in a high demand area. This enables them to retrieve more jobs, which results in longer wait times for their matched passenger.

Like Uber, Lyft and Grab, GO-JEK provides incentive programs for drivers that have achieved a high volume of trips. This prompts many drivers to take advantage of the system by creating fake driver profiles to simulate real trips.
