During the late 1980s and early 1990s, extensive copper and lithium extraction operations were established in the Salar de Atacama area in the interior of the Antofagasta region of Chile's arid north. Initially, Atacameño-Likanantay (Indigenous) peoples had no say in the establishment and operation of mining projects that appropriated their lands and drew extensively on scarce water resources that are essential to Atacameño livelihoods. Their marginalization reflected Chile's complete failure to recognize the rights and interests of its Indigenous peoples. In 1993, the government of Chile passed its Indigenous Act No. 19.253, providing a process for the recognition of Indigenous peoples.1In the years that followed, it established a framework for environmental regulation and, in 2008, it ratified the International Labour Organization (ILO) Indigenous and Tribal Peoples Convention (ILO,1989).2These developments offered hope to Atacameño communities that they would be given an effective voice in the assessment of mining projects and in the allocation of water and other valuable resources, and that they would be allowed to share in the economic benefits of extractive activity on their traditional lands. However, any hope that state action would lead to fundamental change was dashed; the Indigenous Act was implemented in a way that continued to allow unfettered access to miners, environmental assessment and regulation proved to be largely tokenistic and reactive, and government agencies continued to behave in ways that conflicted with the principles underlying ILO Convention 169.
