On the Atacama plateau in northern Chile, at an elevation of roughly 2,300 meters above sea level, SQM operates what is arguably the most productive lithium facility in the world. The Salar de Atacama occupies approximately 3,000 square kilometers of salt flat — an area larger than Delaware — and the evaporation pond systems that extract lithium from its brine cover several hundred hectares of lined surface, with additional area set aside for intermediate concentration stages. From the moment brine is pumped from wells sunk into the aquifer to the moment lithium chloride concentrate reaches the conversion plant, the process takes between twelve and eighteen months. In a good year, about 43 to 47 percent of the lithium originally in the brine becomes product. The rest — more than half — remains in the ground, is evaporated with the water, or is discarded in spent brine streams that cannot be economically recovered.
...
The land footprint of evaporation pond operations is among the largest of any industrial extractive process relative to its annual throughput. A facility producing 20,000 to 40,000 tonnes of LCE per year requires evaporation pond area in the range of 2,000 to 5,000 hectares of actively managed pond surface, plus additional area for buffer zones, infrastructure, and access. The Atacama operations that supply a meaningful fraction of global lithium occupy tens of thousands of hectares of salt flat in aggregate. 

