[10] 11 The Salar de Atacama is used as a case study to analyse and quantify coupled natural 12 (evaporation and recharge) and anthropogenic processes (pumping of lithium-rich brine) to 13 abstract their patterns to other salt flats using a three-dimensional groundwater flow model. 14 Important changes in the dynamics of the water table between the pre-operational period 15 (1986-1994) and operational period (1994-2015) are observed. The water table exhibited a 16 gradual drawdown during the pre-operational period because the evaporation was greater than 17 the recharge for most of these periods. This negative balance was counteracted by some sharp 18 rises that were produced by direct rainfall events on the salt flat. The deep lateral recharge that 19 arrived from the mountains did not produce abrupt changes in the water table because the rain 20 events in the mountains were damped by the distance of the recharge zone and great thickness 21 of the unsaturated zone. 22 The natural evolution of the water table was modified by the intensive brine pumping that was 23 performed in the south-western Salar de Atacama during the operational period. As evaporation 24 depends on the water table depth, the pumping caused a drawdown of the water table, resulting 25 in an evaporation rate reduction that partially compensated for the pumped brine in the water 26 balance of the basin. This effect is defined as the damping capacity of salt flats. Thus, salt flats 27 have a high capacity for dampening oscillations in their water table in response to both natural 28 and anthropogenic disturbances which is of great importance for the management of lake and 29 wetland ecosystems and brine exploitation. The limit of the dampening capacity of salt flats is 30 defined by the evaporation extinction depth, which is in the range of 0.5-2 m. 31
