Lithium extraction technology splits into three families: hard-rock mining of spodumene ore, evaporation of brine in surface ponds, and direct lithium extraction (DLE) — a set of chemical and electrochemical processes that pull lithium ions straight out of brine in hours instead of the 12–18 months evaporation ponds need. Global lithium mine production outside the United States reached 240,000 tonnes in 2024, up 18% year-over-year, according to the USGS Mineral Commodity Summaries 2025. US domestic lithium carbonate production capacity stood at just 5,000 tonnes that same year — a gap that is the entire reason DLE technology is being built out across Arkansas, Nevada, and California. 

