URL: https://alpha.creativecirclecdn.com/editorandpublisher/files/20251016-122943-46c-Reporters%20Shield%20FAQs_2025.docx.pdf
Who is Reporters Shield for? 
The program is predominantly for media outlets and NGOs that report 
in the public interest. This includes NGOs documenting human rights, 
environmental rights & labor rights as well as news platforms that 
include investigative journalism. 
Membership is assigned to organizations, not individuals. However, if 
an individual is subjected to a legal threat or claim linked to content 
published by a Reporters Shield member, they would be supported by 
Reporters Shield for that case, if the member agrees. 
What are the basic eligibility requirements? 
Members must: 
○ be legally registered. 
○ have non-pro fit status or, 
○ declare ownership all the way to ultimate individual bene ficial owner(s). 
○ have transparent financial management. 
○ distinguish clearly between opinion and news reporting. 
○ have editorial independence and professional editorial policies. 
○ be independent from political, commercial, or other in fluence or interference. Can media organizations not registered as non-pro fits also become a member? Yes. For-pro fit media organizations are eligible, provided their ownership is transparent. What kind of content is covered by Reporters Shield? Reporters Shield primarily covers print and online media. We also cover scripted video or audio content, not live, and up to a maximum of 30 minutes. 
How is the membership fee calculated? 
The membership cost is calculated based on a formula developed as a 
result of an actuarial study that analyzes the risk pro file of a 
prospective member. We look at where the organization is based, the 
number of stories published, the cost to defend cases in their country, 
and their history of legal attacks. Members pay an annual membership 
fee plus a deductible for the first four claims. 
