[3] Camera Insurance for Theft from a Car: What's Covered · nextsure (2026-06-18T12:13:33.000Z)
URL: https://www.nextsure.de/en/blog/kameraversicherung-diebstahl-aus-dem-auto
Camera insurance pays out for theft from a car provided it is a provable break-in theft from a fully locked vehicle. It is a strict requirement that the equipment was stored out of sight in a closed area, such as the trunk or glove compartment. The incident must be reported to the police within 24 hours.
...
The basic requirements for cover only apply to a break-in theft from a car under strict conditions. Insurers require that the vehicle was completely locked at the time of the theft. An open window, an unlocked convertible top, or a faulty central locking system will inevitably void the insurance payout. The camera equipment must be in a closed area inside the car. In concrete terms, that means storage in the trunk or glove compartment, out of sight [1]. Items left openly visible on the passenger seat or back seat are categorically excluded from cover. This strict rule protects insurers from careless behavior by policyholders and minimizes moral hazard. That's a hard fact. Anyone who ignores these basic rules loses any claim to financial compensation from the insurer.
...
The distinction from standard home contents insurance reveals significant coverage gaps for a car break-in that many consumers underestimate. A standard home contents policy covers theft of household items from a car through what's known as away-from-home cover, provided the vehicle was forced open [3]. However, extremely strict compensation limits apply here, often just one to two percent of the agreed sum insured. For expensive photo equipment worth several thousand euros, this amount is usually nowhere near enough. In addition, away-from-home cover often only applies during the day or explicitly excludes expensive electronic devices from its terms. A high-performance camera equipment insurance p…

