Although the speech act of apology has been extensively investigated, there has been relatively little research on apologies in Computer-Mediated Communication (CMC). The present study presents a comparative analysis of hotels’ apologies to customers inEnglishand Japanese CMC, drawing upon Spencer-Oatey’s (2008) framework of rapport management. Data consisted of 200 responses by hotels to negative reviews on TripAdvisor. Rhetorical Move Analysis was used to identify Apologies and accompanying moves. Contrary to the perception that Japanese apologize more, results showed considerable similarity in the frequency of apologies and in the tendency to be unclear about accepting responsibility for service failure in English and Japanese. There was also similarity in the use of a corporate voice, but differences in the frequency and use of Explanation, Repair, Opening and Closing moves to enhance rapport.
...
In the present study we examine English and Japanese apologies by hotels in replies to negative reviews on TripAdvisor. Although the speech act of apologizing has been researched extensively, the apologies in TripAdvisor response texts vary in some significant ways from the apologies that have been analyzed in comparative studies of apologies in English and Japanese, and in most other studies of apologies. To begin with, the apologies in these texts are naturally-occurring ones from actual interaction. In this, they differ from the apologies in many cross-cultural studies which rely on data elicited in Discourse Completion Tasks in which a speaker writes what he or she would say in situations that normally call for apologies (e.g., Barnlund and Yoshioka, 1990, Tanaka et al., 2008). There can be a question about the validity of data obtained in this way, as it is not clear…

