Jamaica. The dataset tracks their absorption into enslaver households on a person-by-person basis. Notably, enslavers preferred to categorize captives as fungible or personal property, rather than real estate, when making bequests. This seemingly small legal distinction strongly influenced enslaved people’s lives. Labeling captives as moveable goods intensified their commodification in the marketplace, where they could be used as capital in credit and debt transactions. Women, who customarily inherited moveable goods, also strengthened their claims to enslaved people as personal property. These legal alterations increased the precarities of living in bondage on the island.

