The British Parliament enacted the Molasses Act to impose a duty of sixpence per gallon on foreign molasses imported into North American colonies.

The passage of this legislation was not a random administrative decision but a calculated move by the British Parliament during the reign of King George II. By the early 18th century, the British Empire was operating under a strict mercantilist policy, where colonies existed primarily to enrich the mother country. The 1733 Act was a centerpiece of this strategy, designed to ensure that the lucrative sugar and molasses trade remained within British control.
...
The primary architects behind the bill were the powerful British West Indies sugar planters. These individuals held significant influence within the House of Commons and the House of Lords, forming what was known as the "Sugar Interest." These planters faced stiff competition from French and Dutch Caribbean colonies, which produced molasses at a much lower cost due to more efficient production methods and lower internal taxes.

To protect their profit margins, these lobbyists pressured Parliament to pass protective tariffs that would effectively price out foreign competitors. Their goal was to create trade monopolies that forced New England distillers to buy exclusively from British Caribbean islands, such as Jamaica and Barbados, regardless of the higher market price.
