Artificial Intelligence (AI) technology can be very effective within software development, a field which drives technological progress in a myriad of application domains. AI-driven Development Environments (AIDEs) can help to automate aspects of coding such as typing, translating, problem-solving, testing, and debugging. AIDEs typically offer chat messaging, interactive text editing, or both. Programmers can use AIDEs as addons to their existing Integrated Development Environments to generate new text, edit existing text, learn more about a topic, or take an action (e.g., run a terminal command). AIDEs can produce outputs that are fast, highly effective, and well-liked, but can also produce incorrect or unsafe code Gardella et al. (2024a); Pearce et al. (2022a); Vaithilingam et al. (2022a); Weisz et al. (2021a). As such, it is important to study how programmers can use AIDEs to boost their productivity and well-being without compromising the safety of anyone their software will impact Hoff & Bashir (2015a); Lee & See (2004a); Lindsey et al. (2025a); Sadowski & Zimmermann (2019a). Unfortunately, it is not yet possible to ensure that AIDEs will effectively balance these goals Lindsey et al. (2025a). Instead, human programmers must decide whether and to what extent they should rely on AIDEs. According to the human-automation interaction literature, this decision is largely based on a user’s trust in an automated system Hoff & Bashir (2015a); Lee & See (2004a); Passi & Vorvoreanu (2022a). With this connection, ensuring trust aligns closely with reliability should lead to ideal reliance.

