2.3.2 Estimating AI Code Integration Effort Previous work estimates post-acceptance integration effort at different levels and through different kinds of proxies [32, 9, 33, 34]. Mozannar et al. developed CUPS, a classification of programmer activities when using GitHub Copilot, and measured the time developers spent in those activities, find ing that developers spend half of coding time interacting with the AI system [32]. In follow-up work, they used CUPS-derived measures to build a framework for deciding which suggestions to display or withhold [9]. However, both studies estimate integration effort primarily through time spent in interaction states and do not capture other behavioral events after a developer accepts a suggestion. Another method for estimating integration effort is repository mining, which estimates effort indirectly through later rework visible in version control. Przymus et al. found that ChatGPT-influenced lines are modified or removed sooner [33], while Xiao et al. found no overall increase in code churn after adopting generative AI [34]. Although these approaches capture downstream rework patterns at scale, they do not link effort to a specific accepted suggestion and do not show immediate integration work inside the IDE.

