LoRaWAN (Long Range Wide Area Network) is a low-power, wide-area networking protocol designed specifically for wirelessly connecting battery-operated sensors over distances of 2–15 km. Unlike cellular IoT (NB-IoT, LTE-M) which requires SIM cards and monthly data plans, LoRaWAN operates on license-free ISM bands (EU868, US915, AS923, CN470), giving operators full control over their network infrastructure with zero recurring connectivity fees. For deployments requiring cellular backhaul in areas without Ethernet, our industrial 4G router can serve as the uplink for LoRaWAN gateways.

A single LoRaWAN gateway can serve over 10,000 end devices across a 15km radius, making it exceptionally cost-effective for large-area deployments. Sensor nodes consume as little as 5μA in sleep mode, enabling 5–10 year battery life on a single AA cell. This combination of long range, low power, and high capacity makes LoRaWAN the dominant LPWAN technology for smart agriculture (soil moisture, weather stations), water utility monitoring (remote meter reading, leak detection), environmental sensing (air quality, flood warnings), and asset tracking across industrial campuses.
...
Successful LoRaWAN deployment starts with proper RF coverage planning. In open agricultural terrain, a single gateway mounted at 10m height can cover 10–15km radius with a 6dBi omnidirectional antenna. In urban or industrial environments with buildings and metal structures, typical coverage drops to 2–5km, requiring multiple gateways with overlapping coverage zones for redundancy. Our engineering team provides free coverage simulation using terrain elevation data and link budget calculations for projects above 10 gateways.

