Pilbara dust contains fine silica particles that penetrate IP-rated enclosures through microscopic gaps created by thermal expansion cycles. These particles create conductive paths across insulators when combined with condensation from temperature differentials between day and night operations. A switchboard operating at 35°C internal temperature faces external ambient conditions of 48°C, then drops to 18°C overnight – this 30-degree cycle occurs 365 days per year.
...
Standard IP65-rated enclosures provide adequate protection in controlled environments but require enhanced sealing protocols for Pilbara deployment. Gasket materials must withstand UV degradation and maintain compression properties across temperature ranges from 15°C to 55°C. Silicone gaskets outperform neoprene in these conditions, maintaining seal integrity for 7-9 years versus 3-5 years for conventional materials.
