The P wave marked by the arrow is a sinus P wave because it has the same timing regularity and the same morphology as the other regular sinus P waves. It is, however, not followed by a QRS complex. At first glance, this would appear to be second-degree Mobitz type II AV block. Mobitz type II AV block typically occurs in the His-Purkinje system in the presence of underlying disease manifested as bundle-branch blocks that are not seen on this ECG. It would be unusual for this patient to have such an unexpected block when all the other sinus P waves conduct without any conduction abnormality. A more unifying mechanism for this ECG is the presence of concealed junctional/His-bundle ectopy. As noted, this patient has premature junctional/His-bundle complexes that often block in the right bundle branch. A premature junctional/His-bundle complex could, however, also block in the left bundle branch. In such an instance of block in the distal His-bundle or both the right and left bundle branches, the premature junctional/His-bundle complex is unable to generate a QRS…

