URL: https://digest.law/commercial-and-trade-law/business-transactions-law/buyer-remedies/rejection-of-goods/timing-and-notice-of-rejection/
When a buyer receives a delivery of goods that does not conform to the contract, the Uniform Commercial Code (UCC) gives the buyer a powerful remedy: the right to reject the goods. That remedy, however, is not open-ended. UCC § 2-602(1) requires that rejection occur “within a reasonable time after their delivery or tender” and that it “be ineffective unless the buyer seasonably notifies the seller” (§ 2-602. Manner & Effect of Rightful Rejection). Two independent conditions therefore animate this area of law — a temporal deadline and a communication requirement — and both must be satisfied for a rejection to be legally effective. Failure on either dimension converts a rejection into a wrongful rejection, which then unlocks the seller’s general remedies under § 2-703 (Performance and Remedies).

This issue sits doctrinally between the perfect-tender rule of § 2-601 (which permits rejection for any nonconformity) and the downstream consequences of acceptance, including revocation of acceptance under § 2-608. It is operational law: deadlines and notice letters, not abstract policy. Because § 2-602 is uniformly enacted across U.S. jurisdictions, the timing-and-notice rule is one of the most stable features of Article 2 (N.Y. Uniform Commercial Code Law Section 2-602).
