Some symptoms point toward a medical emergency and warrant going to the emergency room. The Crohn's and Colitis Foundation advises seeking immediate care for heavy and persistent diarrhea, rectal bleeding with blood clots in the stool, constant pain, and high fever. Severe abdominal pain, significant rectal bleeding, and signs of dehydration such as dizziness, very dark urine, or an inability to keep fluids down belong in this category as well. Ulcerative colitis that becomes acutely severe is a well-defined emergency. Acute severe ulcerative colitis is diagnosed when a person passes six or more bloody stools per day along with systemic signs such as fever, a pulse of ninety beats per minute or higher, or anemia, and it requires hospital admission for intensive treatment and monitoring. If your bleeding, pain, or bowel frequency reaches this level, the hospital is the right destination.
...
Two structural complications deserve particular attention because they can progress quickly and may require surgery. Intestinal obstruction is the most frequent complication of Crohn's disease and results from swelling and scar tissue that narrow the bowel. Warning signs include crampy abdominal pain, often with vomiting and bloating, and an inability to pass stool or gas. Toxic megacolon is described as the most serious complication, involving severe dilation of the colon along with high fever and abdominal pain and tenderness. Clinically, it is defined by colonic dilation greater than six centimeters plus systemic findings such as a temperature above 38 degrees Celsius, a heart rate over 120 beats per minute, or a high white blood cell count. Both conditions require immediate medical attention, so go to the emergency room if these patterns appear.
