URL: https://ealyandcompany.com/blog/round-rocks-median-price-hides-a-line-running-through-mayfield-ranch
Rock ISD, an A-rated ... with deep roots ... a long list of Blue Ribbon
...
This is why the median price you see on any national home-value tracker moves depending on which one you check. Redfin's three-month window ending June 2026 put the median sale price at $373,000. Zillow's index landed closer to $398,000 to $412,000 over roughly the same stretch. Movoto's July 2026 figure came in at $446,495. None of these are wrong exactly. They're just averaging across four different school-district markets that happen to share a city name, and the mix of what sold in a given month shifts the blended number around.
...
The price gap tied to that boundary is real and it's not small. Looking at zone-specific data from earlier in 2026, homes in Round Rock ISD attendance areas were running roughly $385,000 to $440,000, while comparable homes inside Leander ISD zones, spanning established Cedar Park neighborhoods and newer Leander subdivisions, were running closer to $430,000 to $510,000 for similar square footage. Apply that pattern to a subdivision like Mayfield Ranch, where the two districts actually meet, and the logic holds at the parcel level: the Round Rock ISD side prices like the rest of that district's zone data, and the Leander ISD side prices like the rest of its zone data.

Zoom out to the full district level and the spread gets even more pronounced. A trailing 12-month look at single-family closed sales across each district as a whole, refreshed in late June 2026, showed a median sold price of $519,000 for homes zoned to Round Rock ISD against $625,000 for homes zoned to Leander ISD. That figure includes areas well beyond Round Rock's city limits, since both districts extend into Cedar Park and other communities, so it's a district-wide comparison rather than a same-c…
