### North Carolina

Under H.B. 259, enacted in September 2023, North Carolina accelerated the reduction of its flat individual income tax rate. Effective January 1, 2025, the tax rate dropped from 4.5 percent to 4.25 percent. The rate is scheduled to decline to 3.99 percent by 2026.

[5] North Carolina vs Tennessee Taxes 2026: Income & Sales Tax
URL: https://www.countrytaxcalc.com/tax-guides/compare/north-carolina-vs-tennessee/
North Carolina charges a flat 3.99% state income tax on 2026 wages (after a $12,750 standard deduction for a single filer), while Tennessee does not tax wages at all, so on $100,000 of wages a single filer pays about $3,481 less state income tax in Tennessee. The trade-off is sales tax: North Carolina's state rate is 4.75% and Tennessee's is 7%, and with local rates added the Tax Foundation puts the population-weighted averages at 7.10% for North Carolina and 9.61% for Tennessee (July 2026). That gap of about 2.5 percentage points on taxable spending is far smaller than the income-tax saving for most wage earners, so Tennessee comes out ahead on these two taxes at every income level in the table. Property tax is set by each county, so it cannot be compared fairly at state level. Figures assume a single filer, wages only, 2026; local rates depend on the exact address.
