The property tax difference between these two cities is the single largest and most durable cost gap between them. Texas has no state income tax, and school funding in particular leans heavily on local property taxes. According to the Texas Comptroller and Travis County's own Taxpayer Impact Statement, effective property tax rates in Travis County typically run between 1.8% and 2.3% of assessed value, depending on the specific school district, whether the property sits inside Austin city limits, and whether a municipal utility district overlaps the parcel.

Tennessee's structure works differently in two ways. First, the state relies more on sales tax revenue. Second, and less widely understood, Tennessee assesses residential property at only 25% of its appraised market value for tax purposes, not the full value. Davidson County's combined city and county tax rate is about $3.25 per $100 of assessed value, per Nashville'…

