In Tennessee, a home's property tax is its appraised value times 25% (the residential assessment ratio) times the local rate per $100 of assessed value. A $1.5M home in unincorporated Williamson County at the 2025 county rate of $1.30 pays about $4,875 a year. City homes add a city rate on top.
...
Rate: $1.49 per $100 of assessed value (Williamson County $1.30 + City of Brentwood $0.19), 2025 tax year. Source: Williamson County Property Tax Rates.
...
Tennessee taxes homes locally. The county assessor appraises the property, and residential property is assessed at 25% of that appraisal. Each taxing body then applies its rate per $100 of assessed value: the county for everyone, plus a city rate inside city limits, plus a special school district rate in parts of Franklin.
...
Appraised value times the 25% residential assessment ratio gives the assessed value. Multiply the assessed value by the combined local rate per $100 (county, plus city and special school district where they apply) and divide by 100.
...
The Williamson County rate for the 2025 tax year is $1.30 per $100 of assessed value, set after the 2025 reappraisal. Homes inside a city pay the city's rate on top. Check the county's official rate page for the current year before relying on any figure.
