My backend skills were the advantage.
What actually mattered:
API design (60% of GenAI is integrations)
Data pipelines (RAG needs solid ETL)
Scaling systems (LLMs at production scale = infrastructure)
Error handling (models fail, systems shouldn't)
The gap I filled:
Learned embeddings (2 weeks)
Understood transformers (1 month)
Played with prompts (ongoing)
My edge:
Already knew distributed systems
Could build reliable APIs
Understood production operations
A case that proved it:
Team of ML PhDs built amazing model. Couldn't deploy it reliably.
Backend engineer didn't improve the model. But made it production-ready in 3 weeks.

The insight: GenAI needs ML knowledge.
But it desperately needs engineering discipline.
The market isn't saturated with ML experts.
It's starving for engineers who can ship reliable AI systems.
Your backend experience isn't irrelevant. It's your superpower.

