Coursera does not display a price on the Specialization page. Enrollment runs on the standard Coursera subscription, so the real cost is a function of how fast you finish — which makes the schedule, not the sticker, the thing to plan around.

Coursera’s own estimate is 2 months at 10 hours a week. Take that as an optimistic figure rather than a promise: it assumes you do not stall in Course 1, and that ten hours a week is genuinely available. If your math is rusty, budget three months and treat the extra month as the cost of doing Course 1 properly instead of skimming it.

Two things reduce the cost legitimately. There is a free preview of each course’s first module, graded work included, and the subscription usually starts with a free trial — genuinely worth using for a week before you commit, precisely because it lets you hit the Course 1 math and find out whether you will finish. And Coursera offers financial aid on this Specialization, which is a real program rather than a discount gimmick, though it requires an application and a wait.

