When someone screening for an AI/ML role opens your top repository, they are checking for five specific things, usually in this order. Most student and early-career profiles pass zero of them.

| Signal | What most ML repos show today | What recruiters actually look for |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Reproducibility | A notebook with pre-run outputs and no environment file | A pinned dependency list, a fixed random seed, and a one-command way to rerun the pipeline and get the same result |
| Model documentation | Explanation scattered across markdown cells inside the notebook | A dedicated model card stating the training data, the metrics, and the known failure modes |
| Automated checks | No workflow files anywhere in the repo | CI that lints the code, runs a smoke test on the training script, and confirms the saved model still loads |
| Evaluation rigor | One accuracy number reported on the same split the model trained on | Multiple metrics, a proper holdout or cross-validation, and a stated baseline to compare against |
| Path to inference | Training code only; the model never leaves the notebook | A small script or API endpoint that loads the saved model and returns a prediction on new input |
