Yesterday I passed the AWS Machine Learning Specialty (MLS) exam, over the past year and a half I earned four AWS certifications. Here is my summary of what I did and what I would have done differently if I started over.
...
Even if your goal is a specialty certification (like MLS), I strongly recommend taking foundational ones first for a solid AWS knowledge base. The order I followed: Cloud Practitioner (CCP), Solutions Architect (SAA), Data Engineer (DEA), and finally Machine Learning Specialty (MLS).

On Reddit, some people claim to pass these exams studying for only a week, I guess it's possible, but not so sure how much you would actually retain on that.
...
- CCP gives a broad AWS overview but only scratches the surface. Skipping it makes advanced exams harder due to the largest number of services.

- SAA was the toughest for me. It covers broad topics, including private networks, which I had no experience with. Challenging but great for cloud architecture best practices.

- DEA is straightforward if you've taken CCP and SAA. It focuses on data storage, management, and analytics. Hands-on AWS experience helps a lot.

- MLS is tricky if you're new to ML. Modeling concepts aren’t too complex, but questions combine technical knowledge with business scenarios. Having CCP and SAA knowledge helps, letting you focus on ML-specific topics.
...
It took me 1.5 years since I took breaks, but looking back, taking them sequentially would've been smoother - knowledge transfers across exams reducing review time.

No single right way to do it, you do you.
