Only a few stand out for reliability or owner satisfaction in CR member surveys 

 By Mary H.J. Farrell 

Updated by Quentyn Kennemer

 Updated February 1, 2025 

 Photo: Getty Images 

 Of nearly two dozen brands, only two earn the highest predicted reliability score in CR surveys. 

Triple-digit temperatures have become the summer norm for some regions of the U.S. Whether or not that’s true in your area, it’s important to pick a central air conditioning system that won’t easily break. Of the nearly two dozen brands judged in CR’s latest member survey, only two stood out with top scores for both predicted reliability and owner satisfaction. Seven additional central air brands earned favorable reliability scores. 

But the data—based on responses from 13,306 members who purchased central AC or ductless mini-split units between 2009 and 2024—suggests that there are many more brands worth considering. There are also some that you should probably pass on if you’re looking to minimize long-term repair and upkeep costs.

Because there are so many variables—including your local climate, your home’s size and design, how the system is installed, and the construction of the ductwork—Consumer Reports doesn’t test central air conditioning systems. Instead, we estimate the reliability of major brands on the market by asking our members about their experiences with the AC systems they purchased and installed.
