If a man quit his job, sold his house, and moved his wife and five children to another state in order to start the new job. One month later, he was informed things weren’t working out and that his job was terminated. Under what theory can he recover damages?
...
A man quit his job, sold his house, and moved his wife and five children to another state to start a new job that was terminated one month later; under what legal theory can he recover damages?

- Check for a written employment agreement or contract; breach of contract could support damages if the employer promised employment.
- Depending on jurisdiction, claims might include promissory estoppel (reliance), wrongful termination in violation of an express contract or public policy, and possibly torts if misrepresentation or fraud occurred.
- Consult a local employment attorney to review facts, documentation, and available remedies and to evaluate state-specific laws (New Jersey may have particular protections).

 Kevin B. Murphy 

 Franchise Foundations, APC • California 

Answered Jun 12, 2010

Any attorney would ask if there is a written employment agreement. That should have been in place before anything happened. Consult with an attorney in your area for specifics. Kevin B. Murphy, B.S., M.B.A., J.D. - Mr. Franchise Franchise Attorney

