[2] John Deere 9RX four-track tractor design | OEM Off-Highway (2016-01-28T14:13:00.000Z)
URL: https://www.oemoffhighway.com/drivetrains/article/12143964/john-deere-9rx-fourtrack-tractor-design
The first thing MacDonald says sets the undercarriage of a four-track tractor apart from a two-track one is the use of a positive drive versus a friction drive system. He explains that a friction drive system is more common for two-track tractors because it provides the most efficient means of transferring power to the ground and minimizing parasitic losses on the large two-track undercarriage. Four-track undercarriages, on the other hand, are smaller in comparison, enabling a positive drive system to be used. The positive drive system is able to run under less track tension than its friction drive counterpart and relies on the mesh of the drive lugs within the track’s drive sprocket to provide the traction necessary to push the track forward.

For the 9RX, John Deere chose to use a larger sized undercarriage than is typically found on four-track tractors to help improve reliability. MacDonald says it’s about 20% larger than the next largest undercarriage design in the industry. “By going 20% larger on an undercarriage, all of our components are larger and as a result they’re turning more slowly, building up less heat at the same ground speed and dissipating that heat more effectively which is going to lead to longer component life,” he explains.
...
To help mitigate the amount of vibrations an operator feels in the cab, the company chose to utilize a two mid-roller design for the track system. Often times three mid-roller designs found on other track systems have the center mid-roller placed directly under the axle which causes impacts or vibrations to be transmitted through the mid-roller directly to the axle and frame, adversely affecting operator ride quality. “By going to a two mid-roller design, we’ve spaced the mid-rollers out on either side of the axle so we im…

