One critical step in establishing a rental rate is defining the cost of equipment ownership and the cost of operating and maintaining equipment. A brief description of the methodology used in calculating ownership and operating cost is presented below. Cost of Ownership includes the cost of depreciation of the equipment due to use and years in service. It also includes an investment cost (i.e. the cost to borrow money to purchase the equipment and/or the lost interest revenue if that money had been invested elsewhere), as well as housing and insurance costs. In addition, a 15 per cent margin (or cushion) is included to cover unexpected incidentals or fluctuations in equipment costs. To generate a suggested rental rate on a $ per hour basis, the cost of ownership was calculated over the life of the equipment, and the total hours of use over that lifespan were estimated. The total cost of ownership was then divided by the total hours of use to generate a suggested rental rate. Operating Costs include repair and maintenance (broken and worn parts, oil, filters, and labour for repair and service) and fuel use. In addition, labour costs are included, as well as a 15 per cent margin (or cushion) to cover unexpected incidents that affect operating costs.
