The region had less than 5% internet penetration. Credit card usage was minimal outside the wealthy. Most people conducted transactions in cash. The postal infrastructure was unreliable, with packages frequently lost or delayed for weeks. And perhaps most critically, there existed a profound lack of trust between strangers attempting to conduct business. How could you trust someone you'd never met to send payment for your item? How could you trust a seller to actually ship what they promised?
...
In the United States, eBay could initially rely on checks in the mail because the postal system was reliable and there existed a basic level of trust between users. This wasn't viable in Latin America. Without a secure way to handle payments, the marketplace would collapse under the weight of fraud and distrust.

This necessity led to the development of Mercado Pago in 2003. Unlike PayPal, which emerged as a solution for eBay but was a separate company (until acquired), Mercado Pago was built from within as an essential component of the marketplace itself. Its initial function was fundamentally about trust—it served as an escrow system, holding the buyer's payment until they confirmed receipt of the item in good condition.

This seemingly simple addition was transformative. By solving the trust problem, Mercado Pago didn't just facilitate transactions—it unlocked the entire concept of online commerce between strangers in the region. Marketplace transaction volume grew rapidly as both buyers and sellers gained confidence in the system.
