This paper examines Brazil’s Pix as a public digital payment infrastructure that reshapes market coordination, transaction costs, and competition in the payments ecosystem. Rather than treating Pix simply as a payment instrument, the study conceptualizes it as an interoperable network that connects consumers, micro-entrepreneurs, informal workers, firms, and financial institutions through a common, instant, and low-cost transactional layer. Drawing on transaction-cost economics, financial-inclusion theory, field-based observations from informal markets and micro-entrepreneurial environments, and supporting secondary evidence, the paper analyzes how Pix changes the conditions of exchange in Brazil. The evidence indicates that instant settlement and lower payment costs reduce cash dependence, improve liquidity, and strengthen the price competitiveness of small businesses operating with narrow margins. These effects are especially relevant in street markets and urban peripheries, where access to low-cost payment tools can determine the speed, security, and continuity of everyday commercial activity. The paper further argues that Pix reduces segmentation in the financial system by allowing low-income users, informal sellers, and large firms to operate within the same interoperable payment network. This convergence expands active participation in digital finance while placing competitive pressure on legacy fee-based payment models, including card networks and conventional bank transfers. By linking payment-system design to network effects, market integration, and the digitalization of informal economic activity, this study contributes to the literature on financial infrastructure and platform competition. The Brazilian case suggests that publicly coordinated instant-payment…

