[1] Algorithmic Management and App-based Delivery Workers in Rio de Janeiro (2025-04-17T00:00:00.000Z)
URL: https://exa.ai/library/publication/0lsdk5n1c3q
The intense competition among workers, stimulated by platforms that continuously recruit new service providers, further weakens workers' bargaining power (Cockayne, 2016) . Moreover, the algorithmic management of work on these platforms imposes additional control over workers, undermining their autonomy. Platforms monitor and rate workers' performance based on metrics such as punctuality and customer reviews, and these ratings can lead to 'deactivations' (van Doorn, 2020) . Despite presenting themselves as neutral intermediaries, platforms exercise significant managerial control over their workers (Masikane and Webster, 2022) , with a notable asymmetry of information regarding the terms of task distribution and remuneration (Srnicek, 2017) . However, the way in which this process of platformisation has materialisedwhether through the type of technological transformations incorporated, discussions around regulation, or social contexts -has revealed deep inequalities and forms of exploitation that affect workers differently at the local level, bringing forth certain particularities to the Global North and South (Antunes, 2019) . In analysing app-based couriers in Brazil, it is essential to consider not only the typical precarisation of labour, but also the historical conditions that shape a labour market characterised by high levels of informality, the absence of social protections, and various forms of racial and gender-based violence that extend beyond the sphere of work. This article seeks to investigate how the digitalisation of deliveries has altered -or in some cases perpetuated -work relations and conditions for food couriers in Rio de Janeiro, Brazil, with particular attention being paid to the intersections between algorithmic governance, gender, and race.
