Financial losses from Pix scams are growing in value, driven primarily by higher-income victims, and the schemes are becoming increasingly professional. The average loss jumped 21% in the first half of 2025 compared to the same period in 2024, reaching R$2,540 in nominal terms. 

 For people in classes A/B—those with monthly household incomes above R$9,000—the average loss was R$10,500, nearly 70% more than in 2024. At the same time, more than half of the cases (65%) now involve funds being directed to corporate accounts rather than individual accounts, up from 42% last year. 

 The data comes from the third edition of the Pix Scams survey, provided exclusively to Valor. The study is conducted by financial fraud intelligence firm Silverguard, based on analysis of 12,197 victim reports filed with the SOS Golpe hotline. The sample reflects the characteristics of the Brazilian population by sex or gender and income. 

 “It’s believed that people in classes A/B are less susceptible to scams, but everyone is vulnerable to the problem. In this group, income is higher and so are the losses, which drives up the overall figures,” says Silverguard CEO and survey coordinator Marcia Netto. 

 Samuel Barros, dean of Ibmec Rio and a specialist in finance and technology, points to increasingly sophisticated scams as one hypothesis for explaining the rise in cases among higher-income individuals. 

 “With the ability to use audio and video that seem more authentic, it’s possible to construct more targeted scams that reach this audience and generate larger amounts. Additionally, there are many cases of scams on marketplaces [online shopping platforms] that appeal to this group.” 

