The informal employment rate in the region was 47.6 per cent in mid-2024, a slight improvement compared to 2023 (48 per cent) and 2019 (48.8 per cent). As in all years following the impact of the COVID-19 pandemic, the recovery in employment in 2024 was still driven by growing informal employment in several countries in the region, raising concerns about the quality of employment. With the exception of Argentina, Brazil and Mexico, informal employment accounted for between 48 and 70 per cent of the net increase in employment over the past year in the remaining countries, reflecting a lack of progress in formalising the workforce. Gender gaps in the labour market persist. In the second quarter of 2024, the female participation rate was 52.1 per cent, significantly lower than the male rate (74.3 per cent). The female employment rate (48.4 per cent) was also 22 percentage points lower than the male rate (70.4 per cent), and the female unemployment rate (7.2 per cent) was higher than the male rate (5.2 per cent). Although participation and employment gaps between men and women have narrowed, progress remains slow and gender gaps continue to affect the labour market.

