Informal employment is one of the most challenging features of labour markets today. While the reduction in informal employment is clearly recognized as part of the Sustainable Development Goals (Target 8.3), our latest review shows that progress in the relevant indicator (Indicator 8.3.1 on proportion of informal employment in non-agriculture employment) has been painfully slow. The incidence of informality remains high, affecting 61 percent of workers worldwide, and is much higher in low-income countries where most workers cannot afford to be unemployed. The aim of this report is to facilitate the development of a comprehensive methodology for assessing the impact and effectiveness of policies on labour markets, employment and social protection, with special attention accorded to labour informality and gender gaps. It is hoped that the study will help improve policy design and enhance protection for informal workers during future crises, with a goal of accelerating their recovery from the pandemic and promoting their formalization. Argentina, a country in which 84 per cent of businesses are small and medium-sized enterprises and where labour informality stands at 43.2 per cent, was chosen as a case study for this report with a view to achieving those goals. Argentina implemented a series of measures to protect labour relations and mitigate the impact of the COVID-19 crisis on employment, business enterprises and incomes. Those measures included a moratorium on layoffs, subsidized salary payments, reductions in social security contributions and the fostering of social dialogue to ensure that measures were implemented in a timely manner. Loans to provide business enterprises with working capital and income transfers were also approved. For informal workers, the focus w…

