Galperin launched MercadoLibre in 1999 during Argentina’s Convertibility era — the peso was pegged 1:1 to the dollar, inflation was controlled, and the dot-com boom made online marketplace models fashionable globally. The early product was simply an auction platform modeled on eBay, connecting Argentine buyers and sellers who had no other mechanism for online commerce. eBay itself recognized the opportunity early enough to become a shareholder, which provided both capital and institutional credibility.

The 2001 Argentine crisis — the corralito, the peg collapsing, a government that froze its citizens’ bank accounts overnight — could have killed the company. Instead it shaped it. The lesson embedded in that experience was that Latin American consumers had fundamental reasons to distrust traditional financial institutions, and that whoever built an alternative trustworthy enough to hold their money would be sitting on something valuable.
...
Latin America in 2003 had low credit card penetration, almost no digital payment infrastructure, and a population that transacted primarily in cash. An auction marketplace where strangers sent each other money and hoped for the best was not a business — it was a prayer. Mercado Pago launched that year not as a fintech ambition but as a feature: an escrow mechanism that held buyer funds until the seller shipped, creating enough trust between strangers to make transactions actually happen.
