The diagnosis of autism can be made when an individual presents with a behavioral profile that meets diagnostic criteria that are not better attributed to global developmental delay or intellectual disability (ID). This diagnosis can be made by a variety of clinician types and through a variety of diagnostic tools but should be made within the context of a complete history, developmental assessment, and physical examination. A diagnosis should be made by using a combination of rating scales administered to parents and, possibly, teachers and/or caregivers; detailed parent interview; and structured behavioral observations. Most clinicians use a variety of standardized...

