Abstract Women and girls with Autism Spectrum Disorder are systematically underdiagnosed, receiving diagnoses an average of 3-5 years later than male counterparts, with many not receiving diagnoses until adulthood. A primary mechanism proposed to explain this diagnostic gap is 'camouflaging'—the effortful masking and imitation of neurotypical social behaviors that reduces the visibility of ASD features to clinicians and parents. This multi-site qualitative study explored diagnostic experiences, camouflaging strategies, and mental health consequences in 34 autistic women (ages 18-52) diagnosed at ≥ 18 years of age, recruited from autism support organizations in the UK, Nigeria, and the Czech Republic. Interpretative Phenomenological Analysis revealed four superordinate themes: (1) 'Invisible Difference'—sustained efforts to appear socially competent through social script memorization, emotion performance, and interest masking; (2) 'The Cost of Fitting In'—profound mental health consequences including anxiety, depression, and identity confusion from sustained camouflaging; (3) 'Finally Named'—late diagnosis as simultaneously validating and grieving; and (4) 'Reclaimed Identity'—post-diagnosis engagement with autistic community and reappraisal of life narrative. Findings inform the need for female-specific ASD screening indicators and diagnostic training highlighting atypical presentations.
