Last week, the U. S. Department of Justice's Office of Redress Administration (ORA) officially closed its doors. During its 10 years of existence, ORA awarded payments of $20,000 to more than 81,800 Japanese Americans. Equally important, a letter of presidential apology accompanied these payments.

These recipients were among the nearly 120,000 people of Japanese ancestry, virtually all of whom were living on the West Coast during World War II, who were assembled at bayonet-point, uprooted from their homes and confined in 10 desolate locations in seven different states from 1942 to 1945. Two-thirds of these prisoners were American citizens by birth; the remainder were their parents who, from 1922 until 1952, were denied the right to become American citizens. None was charged with any crimes against the United States.

