Reparations are measures to remedy the harm caused to victims of violations. As more victim-centred transitional justice measures, reparations are focused on redressing victims’ harm by acknowledging their suffering and providing services, benefits and support to alleviate the consequences of violations. This sits in contrast to demobilisation programmes, vetting or prosecutions, aimed at those who participate in violence and caused such harm. However, reparations can be a means for those responsible to take ownership of their wrongdoing, demonstrating their move away from violence, and rehabilitating or normalising their position in society. Thus reparations in transitional societies are a key avenue to alleviate the harm caused, contribute to their non-repetition and help to stabilise the relationships between victims, affected communities and perpetrators. The 2005 UN Basic Principles and Guidelines on the Right to a Remedy and Reparation for Victims of Gross Violations of International Human Rights Law and Serious Violations of International Humanitarian Law (UNBPG) declare that reparations are intended to ‘promote justice by redress’. 1 Despite their normative guidance, in practice reparations in transitional societies are complex administrative legal constructions, often shaped and entangled with political, social and moral contentions. Importantly reparations in transitional societies do not solely arise out of the need to comply with international norms, but through the pressure of victims with
