 MESSAGE FROM THE ADMINISTRATOR OF REDRESS 

 In 1980, the Congress established the Commission on Wartime Relocation and Internment of Civilians to study the events and circumstances which resulted in the evacuation, relocation, and internment of citizens and permanent resident aliens of Japanese ancestry during World War II. The Commission concluded that these events were influenced by racial prejudice, war hysteria, and a failure of political leadership, and recommended remedial action. The Civil Liberties Act of 1988 (Public Law 100-383) is the result of those findings and recommendations. 

 On August 10, 1988, President Reagan signed the Civil Liberties Act into law. The purposes of the Act are to acknowledge and apologize for the fundamental injustice of the evacuation, relocation, and internment, to make restitution, and to fund a public education program to prevent the recurrence of any similar event in the future. 

 Under the Act, the responsibility for making restitution is given to the Attorney General. In turn, the Attorney General has established the Office of Redress Administration (ORA), which I direct. ORA is charged with making restitution by identifying, locating, an making payment in the amount of $20,000 to each eligibility for payment. Those regulations will be published for public comment in the Federal Register. 

