M ost of the world's coffee, chocolate and palm oil begins on farms that are, on paper, invisible. A smallholder in Sulawesi or Ivory Coast may grow the raw material for a global brand and yet never appear in any record the brand can audit. No plot boundary. No proof of origin. No leverage. Koltiva was built to close that gap.

Founded in Indonesia in 2013 as a cocoa-tracing tool called CocoaTrace, the company has grown into a Swiss-Indonesian agritech that describes itself as "triple tech" - agritech, fintech and climate tech woven together. Its bet is simple: give every farm plot an identity and a data trail, and the whole chain above it becomes accountable.

Today that trail stretches across more than 60 commodities, from the familiar - cocoa, coffee, rubber, palm oil - to the less obvious, including seaweed, tuna and shrimp. The company reports over two million registered producers and thousands of business customers spanning more than 90 countries.

The engine is a platform called KoltiTrace, which knits together the alphabet soup of enterprise systems - MIS, ERP, CRM, GIS, IoT and digital finance - into one place a brand can watch its supply chain move. But software alone rarely reaches a remote farm. So Koltiva pairs it with KoltiSkills: agronomists and field teams who profile producers, map plots, run training and collect the ground-truth data the software depends on.
