Which application date applies to a given company depends on its size. The EUDR does not set its own size test — it relies on the categories already defined in the EU Accounting Directive.

 How company size is determined: Under Directive 2013/34/EU (the “Accounting Directive”), as amended by Commission Delegated Directive (EU) 2023/2775, a company is micro if it does not exceed two of: €450,000 balance sheet total, €900,000 net turnover, or 10 employees. It is small if it does not exceed two of: €5,000,000 balance sheet total, €10,000,000 net turnover, or 50 employees. It is medium if it does not exceed two of: €25,000,000 balance sheet total, €50,000,000 net turnover, or 250 employees — anything above these thresholds is large. For the EUDR’s extended application date, what matters is whether a company was already established as a micro or small undertaking by 31 December 2024; size classification only changes after a company exceeds or falls under the relevant thresholds for two consecutive financial years. 

