This paper defines the Treatment Reactive Average Causal Effect (TRACE) as the total effect of treatment among units that would, if treated, realize a particular post-treatment value. It then bounds this quantity by placing restrictions on the effect in the non-reactive group rather than conditioning directly on the observed post-treatment variable or invoking the strong assumptions of principal stratification or instrumental variables approaches. The three examples—police-perceived race and violence, community policing implementation in Liberia, and canvassing effects on transgender attitudes—show the authors have concrete applied settings in view and want to avoid bias from post-treatment selection. The framing is distinct enough from standard tools that it could be useful for researchers who care about mechanistic or implementation responses but cannot defend the usual point-identification assumptions. The paper does a clear job stating the bias problem with direct conditioning and why existing strategies often demand indefensible restrictions. The applications help make the case that this is meant as a practical tool rather than a purely theoretical exercise. The soft spot is the bounding step itself. If credible restrictions on the non-reactive effect can be justified from observables or mild domain knowledge without essentially assuming away the identification problem, the ranges will be informative. If those restrictions turn out to be as strong as the ones the authors criticize elsewhere, the method mainly relabels the difficulty rather than relaxing it. The abstract does not show the derivations or any simulation checks, so the full paper must demonstrate that the bounds are non-vacuous and rest on weaker conditions. This is for applied causal inference people…

