> Wat Pho (Wat Phra Chetuphon) — the Temple of the Reclining Buddha — charges 300 THB (~€8) for foreign visitors. Thai citizens enter free. Children under 120 cm enter free. Tickets are available at the gate or online in advance at watphobooking.com (minimum 24 hours in advance). Open daily 08:00–18:30. Traditional Thai massage is available on-site at the temple massage pavilions (260–420 THB, walk-in). No advance booking required for the massage. Dress code applies: shoulders and knees must be covered.

Wat Pho (full name: Wat Phra Chetuphon Wimon Mangkhalaram Rajwaramahawihan) is one of Bangkok’s most historically significant temple complexes and the closest major temple to the Grand Palace: a sprawling compound of 91 chedis, 400+ Buddha images, monks’ quarters, and pavilions covering over 8 hectares in the Rattanakosin district. It is older than the city of Bangkok itself, pre-dating the founding of the capital in 1782, and was renovated and expanded extensively by King Rama I and King Rama III. It is home to the most famous single object in Buddhist Thailand: the 46-metre gold-plated Reclining Buddha, and to the Wat Pho Thai Traditional Medicine School — the recognised birthplace of traditional Thai massage, earning a UNESCO Memory of the World listing for its medical knowledge inscriptions.
