### Ride the Death Railway

The surviving section of the Thailand–Burma Railway, built by Allied prisoners of war and Asian labourers in 1942–43, still runs as an ordinary third-class service. From Kanchanaburi it crosses the Bridge on the River Kwai, then edges along the cliff above the Khwae Noi on the wooden Wang Pho viaduct near Tham Krasae before ending at Nam Tok. Fares for foreigners are a flat 100 THB.

Kanchanaburi town's three essentials fit into a morning. Walk the steel bridge over the Khwae Yai, stepping aside for the trains; pay respects at Kanchanaburi War Cemetery, where thousands of Allied prisoners are buried; then spend an hour in the air-conditioned Thailand–Burma Railway Centre on the cemetery's western side, the clearest account of how the railway was built and what it cost. It is the context that makes the train ride mean something.

