TL;DR: Thailand has six domestic airlines split across two Bangkok airports: full-service Thai Airways and Bangkok Airways fly from Suvarnabhumi (BKK), budget carriers AirAsia, Nok Air and Thai Lion Air are based at Don Mueang (DMK), and Thai Vietjet flies budget routes from Suvarnabhumi. Which one you should fly comes down to whether you want a checked bag included (Thai Airways, Bangkok Airways) or the lowest headline fare with baggage bought separately (AirAsia, Nok Air, Thai Lion Air, Thai Vietjet). One-way fares on the busiest routes, Bangkok-Chiang Mai and Bangkok-Phuket, typically run roughly ฿1,000-3,600 (US$30-110) depending on how far ahead you book, while Bangkok-Koh Samui runs higher at roughly ฿2,300-4,800 (US$70-145) one-way because Bangkok Airways owns Samui's airport outright. The single biggest mistake travellers make is confusing Bangkok's two airports, Suvarnabhumi and Don Mueang sit about 25km apart with no shared terminal, so a same-day connection between an international arrival and a domestic budget flight needs real transfer time. Budget carriers rarely include a checked bag on their cheapest fares, buy it online in advance rather than at the airport, where the same weight can cost 30% or more extra and unbought excess baggage runs ฿300-350 (US$9-11) per kilogram. Foreign travellers need a passport for every domestic flight, no exceptions. All prices ฿33 = US$1 (July 2026), and fares and baggage rules change often, so treat the figures here as a guide, not a quote.
