URL: https://outthailand.com/guide/mae-kampong-village/
TL;DR: Mae Kampong is a small, cool-climate village in the hills about 50km east of Chiang Mai (roughly a 1 to 1.5 hour drive), sitting around 1,300 metres above sea level, best known for its community-run tourism model, a multi-tier waterfall, coffee and tea grown on the surrounding slopes, and wooden homestays strung along a mountain stream. You can do it as a day trip or, better, stay a night in a village homestay, which typically runs roughly ฿550-800 (US$17-24) per person including breakfast, according to current homestay and tour listings, confirm the exact rate when you book. There is no gate fee to enter the village itself. Getting there without your own transport, a shared van from Warorot Market runs about ฿200 (US$6) one way and needs booking the day before, a Grab or private taxi runs roughly ฿700-1,200 (US$21-36), or you can rent a scooter or car in Chiang Mai. The village is genuinely cooler than the city, so pack a light layer even in the hot season. All conversions at ฿33 = US$1 (July 2026), and prices change, so treat figures here as a planning range, not a quote.
