Ayutthaya, capital of the Kingdom of Siam from 1351 until its destruction by the Burmese army in 1767, still has dozens of ruined temples on its island and riverbanks: bell-shaped chedis, Khmer-style prangs, headless Buddhas and the famous Buddha head caught in the roots of a fig tree. The historical park has been a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 1991 and can be visited in a day from Bangkok. The air-conditioned SRT Bangkok Connex train links Krung Thep Aphiwat station to Ayutthaya in about 1h15; once there, a ferry crosses the river opposite the station and a bicycle takes you from one temple to the next. Each major temple run by the Fine Arts Department costs 50 baht for foreign visitors, or 220 baht with the combined ticket for six temples.
