Before the how-to, a reminder of what the unit packs. The RØDECaster Pro II has four Neutrik combo inputs for mics, instruments and line sources, driven by its Revolution Preamps (up to 76 dB of gain, very low input noise). The surface offers nine channels — six physical faders and three virtual — eight programmable SMART pads, a Bluetooth channel for phone calls, a built-in wireless receiver compatible with two RØDE Series IV transmitters, four headphone outputs and dual USB-C connectivity. Every channel has full processing (high-pass, noise gate, compressor, de-esser, three-band EQ) and the master bus gets APHEX processing — Aural Exciter, Big Bottom and the Compellor leveller.
...
Recording a stereo mix sets everything in stone: if a guest spoke too loudly, if a mic caught a table bump, there is nothing you can do afterwards. Multitrack records each source on its own track — every mic, the phone channel, the USB channel, Bluetooth, the pads — alongside the main mix. In the edit you ride one person’s level, cut a coughing fit, EQ a single voice without touching the others. It is the same logic that makes a guide like the Tascam Model 12 worthwhile: a production console only earns its place if it hands control back to you in post.

