[1] How To Deal With Humidity When Running Outdoor Shows – New York Audio System (2026-03-29T00:00:00.000Z)
URL: https://www.newyorkaudiosystem.com/how-to-deal-with-humidity-when-running-outdoor-shows/
No environmental force is more insidious to production equipment than high humidity. Unlike rain — which is visible, immediate, and forces decisive action — humidity works invisibly, infiltrating connectors, saturating circuit boards, and degrading signal quality across a show day before anyone on the crew realizes what is happening. The production teams that survive outdoor festival seasons with their gear intact and their reputations undamaged treat humidity management as an operational discipline, not an environmental footnote.

High-humidity environments — coastal festival sites, tropical touring markets, summer amphitheater seasons in the American Southeast, monsoon-season corporate events in Southeast Asia — push equipment to failure modes that never appear in a climate-controlled rental house. Understanding the underlying physics, the failure modes specific to production equipment, and the mitigation strategies that actually work gives your rig a fighting chance when the dew point climbs before noon.
...
Humidity attacks electronics through two primary mechanisms. The first is condensation — when warm, moisture-laden air contacts a surface cooler than the local dew point temperature, liquid water forms directly on that surface. This is the mechanism that destroys consoles left powered off overnight in high-humidity outdoor environments. Internal temperatures drop below the dew point during the early morning hours, and when power is applied to a condensation-coated circuit board, the results range from intermittent failures to immediate catastrophic damage.

The second mechanism is contact corrosion and oxide buildup. XLR connectors, PowerCon terminals, BNC connectors, and signal-path contacts all develop micro-corrosion in sustained high-humidity conditions. Thi…

[2] Why vintage mixing console channel faders develop crackling: contact material oxidation explained – retrotechlab.com (2026-05-16T00:00:00.000Z)
URL: https://www.retrotechlab.com/vintage-mixing-console-faders-crackling-contact-oxidation/
This isn’ ... a loose cable. It’s not the preamp. It’s the fader ... —specifically, the contact materials inside the potentiometer that the fader mechanism drives. And ... -grime ... : the metals that make contact with ... are oxidizing at a molecular level.

Fader crackling is one of the most common failure modes in vintage mixing consoles, and it’s entirely predictable once you understand what’s happening inside the sealed housing. After 35–50 years of operation, even consoles stored in climate-controlled studios develop this problem. The physics is straightforward. The economics of repair are not. And whether you fix it depends on understanding exactly what’s deteriorating and why.
...
The atmosphere inside a sealed potentiometer housing is not pure nitrogen or an inert gas. It’s the air that was sealed in when the unit was manufactured. That air contains oxygen and moisture. Over 40 years, even in a climate-controlled studio, the metal surfaces oxidize very slowly.

When beryllium copper or phosphor bronze oxidizes, a thin layer of metal oxide—primarily copper oxide—forms on the surface. This happens at a glacial pace in dry conditions, but it happens. The oxide layer is only a few nanometers thick at first, invisible to the naked eye. But electrically, it’s significant.

Copper oxide has a much higher electrical resistance than the underlying metal. When the wiper moves across the resistive track, it’s no longer making continuous, full contact with the conductor. Instead, the wiper must break through this oxide layer as it moves. That mechanical breaking-through, combined with the high-resistance oxide itself, generates noise in the audio circuit.
