Technology has come a long way, but it’s still prone to failure. Common culprits like OS updates, SSD corruption, and sudden power surges can end a session in seconds. Many creators rely solely on cloud recording, but this is a high-risk strategy for business-critical content. If your local network flinches, the cloud recording might stutter or cut out entirely. This is why the principle of redundancy is so vital. Redundancy in podcasting is the intentional duplication of recording paths to ensure that a single technical failure cannot result in the loss of the entire session.
